- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a branchless, efficient 128-bit bit-count using a proven 64-bit SWAR approach on two halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Treat 128-bit as two independent 64-bit parts
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example
